{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "From camera to words: how Signal works",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "docs/CODE_GUIDE.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Welcome",
      "description": "This tour follows the data from the camera to the words on screen, in about 40 stops. Use the arrows (or Ctrl+Right) to move between steps. The same explanations are in docs/CODE_GUIDE.md."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/main.tsx",
      "line": 6,
      "title": "Where the app starts",
      "description": "**1. Entry point and app state**\n\nThe browser loads index.html, which runs this file. It mounts the React component App into the page."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/App.tsx",
      "line": 31,
      "title": "App: the owner of shared state",
      "description": "**1. Entry point and app state**\n\nApp holds the settings, the sign library, the caption words and the transcript, and passes them to the panels. It creates ONE SignEngine, which does all the real-time work outside React.\n\n**In class:** Separation of concerns: the interface (React) never runs the vision code itself. That keeps the video loop fast."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/App.tsx",
      "line": 101,
      "title": "Receiving recognized words",
      "description": "**1. Entry point and app state**\n\nEvery time the engine decides a sign was made (\"commit\"), this callback appends the word to the caption."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/App.tsx",
      "line": 105,
      "title": "Ending a sentence",
      "description": "**1. Entry point and app state**\n\nWhen the hands have been down for the chosen pause, the current words become a sentence: shown above the caption, added to the transcript, and read aloud if enabled."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/state/engine.ts",
      "line": 192,
      "title": "Starting the camera",
      "description": "**2. The real-time loop**\n\nAsks the browser for the webcam (getUserMedia) at 640x480 or 1280x720, 30 fps, then loads the hand tracker and starts the loop. Camera errors are translated into clear messages (blocked, missing, busy)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/state/engine.ts",
      "line": 298,
      "title": "One step per camera frame",
      "description": "**2. The real-time loop**\n\nrequestVideoFrameCallback fires exactly once per NEW camera frame. Using it instead of a timer means no frame is processed twice and none is waited for.\n\n**In class:** Synchronizing processing with frame arrival minimizes latency: this is the \"no delay\" requirement."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/state/engine.ts",
      "line": 354,
      "title": "The whole pipeline for one frame",
      "description": "**2. The real-time loop**\n\nRead top to bottom: detect hands (and body) -> build normalized features -> smooth -> decoder decides if a sign happened -> words are committed -> skeleton drawn -> speed measured. React only receives a summary 10 times per second.\n\n**In class:** This function is the pipeline diagram from the slides, in code."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/tracker.ts",
      "line": 60,
      "title": "Loading the hand tracker",
      "description": "**3. Perception: from pixels to points**\n\nLoads MediaPipe Hand Landmarker (palm detector + landmark model) on the GPU. If the GPU is not available, it falls back to the CPU. Files load from the app itself first (offline), then from the internet.\n\n**In class:** Two-stage detector: palm detection (SSD-style CNN) then landmark regression (21 keypoints)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/tracker.ts",
      "line": 126,
      "title": "Detecting hands in a frame",
      "description": "**3. Perception: from pixels to points**\n\nReturns 21 points (x, y, z) per hand plus which hand it is (left/right). Timestamps must strictly increase because MediaPipe tracks hands between frames."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/tracker.ts",
      "line": 95,
      "title": "Body tracking",
      "description": "**3. Perception: from pixels to points**\n\nPose Landmarker gives 33 body points. Only enabled for the built-in ASL model, which needs to know where the hands are relative to the face and shoulders."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/lighting.ts",
      "line": 28,
      "title": "Low-light boost",
      "description": "**3. Perception: from pixels to points**\n\nIn a dark room the tracker receives a brightened copy of the frame (brightness gain up to 2.4x plus a little contrast). Brightness is measured twice per second on a 32x24 thumbnail, and the boost switches on below 75/255 and off above 95/255.\n\n**In class:** Image enhancement before detection: a classic preprocessing step. The two thresholds are hysteresis again."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/continuity.ts",
      "line": 27,
      "title": "Bridging tracking gaps",
      "description": "**3. Perception: from pixels to points**\n\nA hand lost for up to 150 ms (about 4 frames, typically motion blur) is kept at its last position, drawn faded. A hand that reappears under the other left/right label is not duplicated.\n\n**In class:** Temporal coherence: objects do not vanish between two frames, so short detection failures are filled in from the past."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/state/engine.ts",
      "line": 330,
      "title": "Self-repair",
      "description": "**3. Perception: from pixels to points**\n\nIf 8 frames fail in a row (graphics card reset after sleep, driver problem), the tracker is rebuilt. A second failure within a minute switches to the processor."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/framing.ts",
      "line": 21,
      "title": "Framing advice",
      "description": "**3. Perception: from pixels to points**\n\nChecks the landmarks already found: hand touching the border, hand too small (far) or too large (close), shoulders missing for the ASL model. A hint appears only after 0.7 s, so it does not flicker."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/features.ts",
      "line": 26,
      "title": "Two hand slots",
      "description": "**4. Representation: from points to features**\n\nEach frame has slot 0 (signer's right hand) and slot 1 (left hand). If both hands claim the same side, the more confident one keeps it."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/features.ts",
      "line": 41,
      "title": "Normalization: the key idea",
      "description": "**4. Representation: from points to features**\n\nx is multiplied by width/height (aspect correction), every point is measured from the wrist (translation invariance) and divided by the hand size, wrist to middle knuckle (scale invariance).\n\n**In class:** Invariance by design, like SIFT: the same handshape gives the same numbers anywhere in the image, near or far."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/features.ts",
      "line": 62,
      "title": "Comparing two handshapes",
      "description": "**4. Representation: from points to features**\n\nRoot mean square distance between matching landmarks, in hand sizes. 0 = identical; about 0.3 and above = clearly different shapes."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/features.ts",
      "line": 94,
      "title": "Measuring motion",
      "description": "**4. Representation: from points to features**\n\nSpeed in hand sizes per second: wrist travel plus a bit of handshape change. This drives the decoder (still vs moving)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/features.ts",
      "line": 114,
      "title": "Mirroring",
      "description": "**4. Representation: from points to features**\n\nSwaps left and right hands and flips x. Used so a sign taught with one hand is also recognized with the other (data augmentation at recognition time)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/smoothing.ts",
      "line": 28,
      "title": "One Euro filter",
      "description": "**4. Representation: from points to features**\n\nEstimates how fast the value changes; fast change -> high cutoff frequency -> little smoothing (no lag); slow change -> strong smoothing (no jitter).\n\n**In class:** An adaptive low-pass filter (Casiez et al., 2012): the classic answer to the jitter vs latency trade-off."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/decoder.ts",
      "line": 101,
      "title": "The decoder state machine",
      "description": "**5. Time: when does a sign happen?**\n\nCalled for every frame. States: idle (no hands), hold (hands still), moving. Each state triggers a different recognizer.\n\n**In class:** Temporal segmentation of a continuous stream: there is no start button in real signing."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/decoder.ts",
      "line": 129,
      "title": "Hysteresis",
      "description": "**5. Time: when does a sign happen?**\n\nMore motion is needed to START moving than to KEEP moving (threshold vs 0.6 x threshold). This prevents flickering between states when the speed hovers around the threshold.\n\n**In class:** Same idea as a Schmitt trigger in electronics, or the two thresholds of the Canny edge detector."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/decoder.ts",
      "line": 162,
      "title": "Still signs need a stable answer",
      "description": "**5. Time: when does a sign happen?**\n\nA word is only written if the same confident answer is held for the hold time (400 ms by default), and not repeated until the hand changes."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/decoder.ts",
      "line": 184,
      "title": "Moving signs: classify the whole movement",
      "description": "**5. Time: when does a sign happen?**\n\nWhen the hands stop or leave, the collected frames form a segment, classified as one sign. The segment is also reported to the built-in ASL model."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/recognizer.ts",
      "line": 44,
      "title": "Choosing example frames",
      "description": "**6. Recognition from your own examples**\n\nFrom each recorded take of a still sign, keep evenly spaced frames from the middle 60%, skipping the moments the hand arrives and leaves."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/recognizer.ts",
      "line": 110,
      "title": "Automatic thresholds",
      "description": "**6. Recognition from your own examples**\n\nLeave-one-out: compare each example with the other examples of the same sign. Consistent signs get a strict threshold, looser signs a wider one.\n\n**In class:** Per-class calibration from intra-class variance, computed instantly, no training needed."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/recognizer.ts",
      "line": 140,
      "title": "k-nearest neighbors",
      "description": "**6. Recognition from your own examples**\n\nDistance to every stored frame; each sign is scored by the average of its 3 closest examples.\n\n**In class:** k-NN is the simplest classifier and perfect for few-shot learning: adding a sign is just storing examples."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/recognizer.ts",
      "line": 178,
      "title": "Saying \"I don't know\"",
      "description": "**6. Recognition from your own examples**\n\nDistances become probabilities with a softmax that includes an extra \"unknown\" option at ratio 1. If no sign is clearly closer than its threshold, \"unknown\" wins.\n\n**In class:** Open-set recognition: a classifier must be able to reject inputs that belong to no class."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/sequence.ts",
      "line": 30,
      "title": "Preparing a movement",
      "description": "**6. Recognition from your own examples**\n\nPositions become relative to where each hand started (in hand sizes), and the recording is resampled to 24 steps."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/sequence.ts",
      "line": 95,
      "title": "Dynamic Time Warping",
      "description": "**6. Recognition from your own examples**\n\nDynamic programming: cost[i][j] = distance(a_i, b_j) + min(three neighbors). The best alignment lets a slow and a fast version of the same sign match. The band limits how far time can be stretched, which also makes it faster.\n\n**In class:** DTW was the standard in speech recognition before deep learning, and it needs no training."
    },
    {
      "file": "training/train_asl.py",
      "line": 91,
      "title": "Training features (Python)",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nEvery point relative to the middle of the shoulders and divided by shoulder width, plus each hand relative to its own wrist. 182 numbers per frame."
    },
    {
      "file": "training/train_asl.py",
      "line": 158,
      "title": "Data augmentation",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nEach training example is randomly mirrored, rotated, stretched, shifted, sped up or slowed down. The network learns the sign, not one exact recording.\n\n**In class:** Augmentation fights overfitting: with 21 signers, the model must generalize to new people."
    },
    {
      "file": "training/train_asl.py",
      "line": 220,
      "title": "Convolution block",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nDepthwise 1D convolution over time (kernel 17 frames): detects local motion patterns. Residual connection and LayerNorm make deep stacks trainable."
    },
    {
      "file": "training/train_asl.py",
      "line": 233,
      "title": "Attention block",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nSelf-attention: every frame looks at every other frame, so the network relates the beginning and end of a sign. Padding frames are masked out.\n\n**In class:** Convolution = local context, attention = global context. Same mix as in modern speech models (Conformer)."
    },
    {
      "file": "training/train_asl.py",
      "line": 305,
      "title": "Training loop",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nAdamW optimizer, mixed precision on GPU, label smoothing, warm-up then cosine learning rate. Validation uses 2 signers never seen in training: 74.1% top-1, about 90% top-5."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/asl/features.ts",
      "line": 93,
      "title": "Same features in TypeScript",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nLine-by-line copy of the Python function. The parity test (asl.test.ts) proves both give the same numbers on real recordings.\n\n**In class:** Training/serving skew is a classic production bug: we test against it."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/asl/features.ts",
      "line": 149,
      "title": "Even rounding matters",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nnumpy rounds 2.5 to 2 (half to even); JavaScript rounds it to 3. Without this function, a few frames would be picked differently from training."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/asl/model.ts",
      "line": 98,
      "title": "Fixing domain shift",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nThe training videos were filmed on vertical phones, so their x axis is stretched about 1.7x compared with a webcam. Comparing body proportions (nose to shoulders vs shoulder width) in the training data and live on camera gives the correction factor.\n\n**In class:** Domain shift: training and real data differ. Here it is measured from the data and corrected geometrically."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/asl/model.ts",
      "line": 110,
      "title": "Inference with test-time augmentation",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nThe sign and its mirror image are both scored and averaged, so left- and right-handed signing work. Runs with ONNX Runtime (WebAssembly) in 20-50 ms."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/state/engine.ts",
      "line": 437,
      "title": "Connecting the network to the live stream",
      "description": "**7. The built-in ASL neural network**\n\nWhen the decoder reports a finished movement that your personal library did not recognize, the matching frames are sent to the network. The word is written if its probability passes the threshold."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/components/TeachPanel.tsx",
      "line": 75,
      "title": "Recording a take",
      "description": "**8. Teaching new signs**\n\nCountdown, then frames are collected for the chosen duration. Takes without visible hands are flagged so they can be redone."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/teach.ts",
      "line": 45,
      "title": "Still or moving?",
      "description": "**8. Teaching new signs**\n\nA take counts as moving if the wrist travels more than 0.6 hand sizes or the handshape changes noticeably. The majority of takes decides."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/core/teach.ts",
      "line": 73,
      "title": "Warning about similar signs",
      "description": "**8. Teaching new signs**\n\nRuns the new examples through a recognizer built from the existing library. If they would be recognized as another sign, the user is warned before saving."
    }
  ]
}